e  wind he says but the water battered  everything in its path Carlos Neen is a  village elder he tells us what he  remembers at the moment the storm hit my  experience is I stay in my house while I  was staying my house the cyclone was  striking the fish steel at her head the  one of the roofing flew out from the  other pelty I need my house that's the  time when I was started to find my home  way to save my life so at the time when  the cyclone would hit my the law or the  law under the roof over  I need my house I was thinking I was  part of my house and safe in other  places so at the time I went up so when  see one of my family come on and his son  fighting in their house so I know at all  the oven that though I went in and  that's where I say so three of us  staining the house and the same time we  see the timber moving we get to himand  nail started to nail the timber some of  the things that happen inside the house  you see it's early in the morning so the  heat is still bearable in the course of  the day temperatures will go up to over  40 degrees Celsius the village chief  takes us to the community center which  miraculously survived more or less  intact this is where locals keep the aid  supplies that have arrived so far the  chief tells us that for the time being  the aid workers have only brought a few  dozen liters of drinking water and sixth  are pollens yeah only under litres of  water not in an touched it that's family  he's disappointed by a organisations and  the government which promised rapid  relief he feels he's been left in the  lurch the water supplies will only last  another few days Roger and his family  lived close by his friends are helping  him rebuild his home but he can only get  building materials in the capital and he  can't afford them anyway Roger is a  fisherman he makes his living selling  barracudas and tuna fortunately his boat  survived the cyclone unscathed but  before he can return to work the village  needs to be cleaned up sand and garbage  washed up in the flood is a major  problem in some places it's half a meter  high  Roger is digging his way through the  sand in the hope of finding at least  some of his and his family's belongings  everything they owned is buried under  the sand like spoons and blades and fork  and some pros are you missing some some  precious things some things of value  yeah like cherries and for my wife Roger  and his family are relying on the  kindness of others they have few savings  and the money they do have they need for  food he's bought rice and fish  one of his daughters finds a photograph  that means a lot to him and his wife  you found it it's a picture of my my  family but she found it and gave it to  me  everyone here is doing their best to  make do and return to normality as soon  as possible but many families are still  having to sleep outside  a few turbulence have been set up here  and there mainly as protection against  the scorching Sun right now the most  urgent problem is the lack of drinking  water  only a few leaders are left  locals have resorted to boiling water  from the nearby river Roy is one of  Rogers neighbors this is the second  severe cyclone he's experienced the  second just a take away my planet half  of my house so I tried to repent for my  house again so there's no enough  material but we try to look for all nail  and Timbers too trendy  even children are helping with the  reconstruction effort for example  collecting useful bits and pieces to  help rebuild  there's no school for the time being  every single school in the region was  destroyed by cyclone Pam here in Takara  locals think it will take four to six  weeks to repair the worst of the damage  but many families tell us they wonder if  it makes sense to rebuild their homes  they're increasingly worried about  living so close to the coast climate  change means water levels are rising how  many more years can the village survive  here  the village chief and the villa